This is a substantial design move. Let me work through what it accomplishes and where the tensions live.

Why this is structurally strong
Several things click that weren't clicking before:

It exponentially expands the T4 design space. Current: roughly 7 strategies times 35 forms equals 35 T4 instances. Proposed: 3 chains times 2-3 T4 options per chain times 2 scope options (character-wide vs chain-wide) equals 12-18 T4 configurations per form, with one selected. Across 35 forms, that's 420-630 distinct T4 configurations rather than 35. Even if many configurations are mechanically similar, the surface area for variance is genuinely orders of magnitude larger.

It makes T4 a build expression rather than a build property. Currently, T4 is something the engine decides for the player. The form 031 player gets "Ironpoint Convergence" because the engine chose it. With your proposal, T4 is something the player chooses among options, which creates player agency at a key build-defining moment. The kit becomes "this character with these options" rather than "this character with this fixed top."

The chain-wide scope option creates genuinely different build feels. A character-wide T4 affects everything the character does. A chain-wide T4 makes one chain dramatically more effective while leaving others normal. These produce qualitatively different play patterns. The character-wide version is a generalist enhancement; the chain-wide version is a specialist commitment. Players choosing between them are making meaningful character-defining decisions.

It aligns naturally with the skill investment progression that Insight C requires. If players spend points to unlock chains, and chains end in T4 choices, the natural progression becomes "specialize in this chain, then choose how its T4 expresses." This is recognizable ARPG progression structure (Path of Exile's ascendancy choices work somewhat like this; Diablo 3's paragon system has chain-style depth choices).

It solves the form 031 / form 034 duplicate problem structurally. When T4s are chain-attached, the hunter form and the warlord form would have T4s for their respective chains — chains that have different mechanical identities (a hunter focus chain vs a warrior rage chain). The same strategic concept (GEOMETRY_COLLAPSE) applied to different chains produces structurally different T4s because the chain context constrains what the T4 affects.

The cycling during simulation gives you balance validation across the expanded space. This is the load-bearing piece. Without simulation cycling through the full T4 option space per form, you'd have variance but no confidence in balance across that variance. With simulation, every T4 configuration that ships has been validated to produce kits within the win rate band.

Where the tensions live
A few things worth working through carefully because they're not obvious from the proposal:

Computational cost expands significantly. Currently the simulation converges on one T4 per form. Your proposal cycles through multiple T4 options times multiple scope settings times multiple skill point allocation routes. Even if "relatively random" means sampling rather than exhaustive enumeration, the convergence work per form grows substantially. Gandalf already flagged the cost implication of per-candidate narration as a 10-times cost multiplier. The simulation cycling may have similar amplification.

You'll want to be precise about how many configurations actually get simulated per form. Is 120 routes per form total? Or 120 per T4 option times 2-3 T4 options times 2 scope settings, which is 720+ routes per form? The compute budget shifts dramatically depending on the answer.

The "chain-wide vs character-wide" scope is a new mechanical concept that needs careful design. It's a great variance generator but it introduces a balance dimension that ARPGs typically don't have. A character-wide 20% damage bonus vs a chain-wide 50% damage bonus — these are presumably balanced to be roughly equivalent in different conditions, but defining that equivalence is non-trivial.

Some questions worth answering: Are chain-wide T4s strictly more powerful within their chain than character-wide ones? If so, what's the tradeoff for taking the character-wide option (presumably broader applicability)? How do chain-wide T4s interact with players who use multiple chains in their rotation? Does a chain-wide T4 implicitly punish off-chain skill usage? These design questions matter and the answers shape whether the variance feels meaningful or feels like noise.

Player decision quality depends on understanding the difference. If a player can't tell whether they should take the character-wide T4 or the chain-wide T4, the choice feels random. Communicating the trade-off clearly — through UI, tutorial, spirit guide narration — is real design work. This isn't a critique of the proposal; it's a flag that the variance creates a player education problem that needs solving alongside the mechanical design.

The cohesion judge gets a harder job. Currently the cohesion judge validates that the T4 narration fits the kit. With your proposal, the cohesion judge needs to validate that the selected T4 from among the options fits the kit, that the narration for that T4 is appropriate, AND potentially that the alternative T4 options would also be appropriate (since players might select them). The judge's evaluation surface expands.

