What you're actually trying to balance
Let me restate the problem precisely because the framing matters:

You want 85% of endgame players, across all gear slots and all rarity tiers, to reach a state where: no combination is game-breakingly powerful (so all combinations stay within an acceptable performance band), AND no combination significantly underperforms the others (so no acquired gear feels wasted relative to alternatives).

This is a harder problem than it sounds because it's actually three problems entangled:

The combinatorial explosion problem. If you have 10 gear slots and each slot has even 5 viable options, that's 9.7 million combinations. If options have rarity tiers (common, magic, rare, epic, legendary), the space multiplies further. You can't balance combinations by enumerating them; you have to balance the contributions in ways that combinations stay within bands regardless of which specific items are equipped.

The progression problem. Endgame gear has to be more powerful than mid-game gear (otherwise progression feels hollow). But endgame gear can't be so powerful that it trivializes endgame content (otherwise progression feels broken). The acquisition curve has to align with the difficulty curve. This is what makes most ARPGs eventually break at high levels — power outpaces content design.

The synergy problem. Gear in ARPGs rarely contributes independently — items combine multiplicatively with skills, with each other, and with character build choices. A 20% damage bonus item paired with a 20% damage bonus skill produces 44% increased damage, not 40%. Three such items produce 72.8% increased damage, not 60%. Multiplicative stacking creates exponential power curves that linear balance attempts miss.

Your engine's situation makes this both harder and easier. Harder because you have 35+ kits each potentially using different gear combinations, so the surface area is enormous. Easier because you have a simulation infrastructure that can validate combinations empirically rather than only theoretically.

How traditional ARPGs solve this (with known failure modes)
Worth knowing what's been tried so you can choose what to adopt and what to avoid:

Diablo 2 used the "items have absolute power" approach. A unique item had specific stats. Balance was achieved through scarcity (powerful items dropped rarely) and through some items being clearly better than others (Stone of Jordan for casters, for example). Failure mode: a small set of items dominated, the rest were trash, and the game became "farm for the specific items that matter." Most ARPGs since have inherited this problem.

Diablo 3 used the "items have variable rolls" approach. A legendary item had a base power level plus randomized affixes. Balance was at the affix level — each affix contributed a small bounded amount, and items had ~6 affixes, so total power was bounded. Failure mode: itemization became a stats game where players optimized affix rolls, and the "interesting" affixes that defined builds dominated everything else. The game became "farm for perfect rolls on the affixes that matter."

Path of Exile uses the "items as platforms for synergy" approach. Base items are relatively weak; player skill expression comes through how items interact with passive tree, with skills, with each other. Balance is achieved by making the interaction space large enough that no single combination dominates because there are too many viable alternatives. Failure mode: complexity explosion where understanding what gear is good requires deep system mastery; the gear-balance question becomes "is the interaction balanced" rather than "is the item balanced."

Last Epoch uses the "items have explicit roles" approach. Different gear slots and rarity tiers have explicit design intents — some slots are for defensive stats, some for damage, etc. Within those roles, items can vary, but cross-role substitution is limited. Balance is achieved by constraining what each slot does. Failure mode: limits player creativity and creates predictable gearing where everyone targets the same stats per slot.

None of these is purely better; each makes different tradeoffs. Worth understanding which tradeoffs match your design goals.

