A proposed gear balance framework
Working through this from your engine's capabilities, here's a framework that might fit:

Step 1: Define gear contribution dimensions explicitly.

Rather than items having arbitrary affixes, define a fixed set of contribution dimensions that all items operate within. Something like:

Damage contribution (offensive output)
Defense contribution (damage mitigation/avoidance)
Sustain contribution (resource regeneration, healing)
Utility contribution (mobility, control, special effects)
Synergy contribution (effects that scale with other items or skills)

Every item, regardless of rarity or slot, contributes to one or more of these dimensions. Higher rarity items contribute more or contribute to more dimensions, but the dimensions themselves are stable.

This isn't innovative — most games do this implicitly. The innovation is making it explicit and using it as the basis for balance measurement.

Step 2: Establish per-slot contribution budgets.

Each gear slot has a defined contribution budget — the total amount of "balance points" the slot can contribute across all dimensions. A weapon slot has a high budget (it's primary). A ring slot has a lower budget. The budget is consistent across rarities for that slot — what changes with rarity is how the budget is distributed, not how much budget exists.

For example, a common weapon might have 100 budget points all in damage. A legendary weapon might have 150 budget points (50% more due to rarity) distributed across damage (80), utility (40), and synergy (30). The legendary is more flexible and slightly more powerful, but not dominant.

The 50% rarity-tier bonus is arbitrary; you'd calibrate it to your desired progression curve. The principle is that rarity provides more options and moderately more power, not radically more power.

Step 3: Run simulation-driven contribution validation.

Here's where your engine's capabilities come in. For each generated item, run simulation against representative kits and content. Measure:

Does the item's contribution stay within its declared dimension budget?
Does the item, when equipped on suitable kits, produce performance within the win rate band?
Does the item, when equipped on unsuitable kits, produce performance below the band but above a minimum floor?
Does the item, in combination with other commonly-equipped items, produce stacking effects that exceed the band?

The simulation validates these empirically. Items that violate the budget get regenerated. Items that produce game-breaking combinations get flagged for inspection.

Step 4: Generate gear in slot-and-build-aware fashion.

When generating gear for a kit, the engine knows what slots need filling and what the kit's build intent is. Generated items target the contribution profile that suits the kit. A defensive tank doesn't generate damage-focused legendary weapons unless that creates an intentional build option. A glass cannon doesn't generate defensive-focused legendary chest pieces unless that creates an intentional build option.

This is more constrained than traditional ARPG drop tables. The tradeoff: less random surprise but more reliable balance. For your auto-combat design, this is the right tradeoff because the items the player gets need to be playable with their build, not just acquirable.

Step 5: Build a balance dashboard that tracks the 85% target.

Across all generated gear, all kits, all content, measure: what percentage of gear-and-kit combinations land in the win rate band? Start with 50-60% and iterate up. Your 85% target is achievable but requires deliberate optimization — it's the kind of metric you tune toward over many simulation runs and content adjustments.

The dashboard becomes the central balance instrument. Every change to gear generation, skill design, or content difficulty gets evaluated against the 85% target.

Specific design decisions worth making
A few decisions that shape how this framework operates in practice:

Linear vs. multiplicative stacking. Multiplicative stacking is what makes traditional ARPGs eventually break. If your gear contributions stack linearly (a 10% damage bonus + 10% damage bonus = 20% damage), you avoid the exponential power curve. If they stack multiplicatively (10% × 10% = 21% combined), you create the curve that requires diminishing returns or other corrections.

For your engine's balance target, linear stacking is significantly easier to keep within a band. Multiplicative stacking is more "exciting" for players (combos feel impactful) but harder to balance. A reasonable middle ground: linear stacking within a dimension, multiplicative only across dimensions (damage × defense doesn't combine; damage + damage just adds).

Power scaling with item level vs. with affix rolls. Diablo 3 scales item power with both level and roll variance. The variance creates the "grind for perfect rolls" problem. Diablo 2 scales primarily with item identity (Stone of Jordan is what it is). Each approach has its tradeoffs.

For your engine, scaling primarily with item level (and keeping roll variance narrow) is probably the right call. Roll variance creates a long tail of imperfect items that feel bad even when they're statistically equivalent. Narrow variance means "I got the legendary" is more meaningful than "I got an imperfect legendary that needs to be rerolled."

Set bonuses and unique mechanics. Set items (gear designed to be worn together for bonuses) and unique mechanic items (items that change how skills work) are where balance gets hardest. They're also where build identity gets strongest.

A reasonable approach: limit set bonuses to small bounded effects (10-20% within a single dimension) rather than build-defining (300% damage, change skill behavior). Limit unique mechanics to items where the mechanic is sized to roughly equal a small affix budget rather than dwarfing all other gear contributions.

This produces less spectacular itemization than Diablo 3 or Path of Exile, but dramatically more stable balance. For an auto-combat game where balance is critical to player satisfaction, the trade is probably right.

Endgame vs. mid-game item differentiation. Your concern is specifically about endgame. What makes endgame items endgame? Options:

Higher contribution budget (more powerful within the framework)
Access to more dimensions (more versatile)
Special properties that mid-game items don't have (specific mechanics)
Higher quality of roll variance (more consistent rolls)
All of the above

The cleanest answer is some combination of higher budget (maybe 30-50% more than mid-game) plus access to more dimensions (legendaries contribute to 3-4 dimensions while commons contribute to 1-2). Special properties on top of that, but kept bounded.

