This is a sharp design move and worth thinking through carefully because what you're describing has real precedent in successful ARPG design, but it also has well-known failure modes that are worth understanding before committing.

What you're proposing
The gear power growth rate exceeds the monster scaling growth rate, so as players push deeper into endgame content (especially infinite tower content), they feel progressively more powerful relative to the content they're facing. The gear-power slope is steeper than the monster-power slope.

This creates a satisfying loop: each tower floor gives the player better gear, which makes them feel meaningfully stronger, which lets them push higher, which gives them better gear, and so on. The 15% hardcore players who push past the 85th percentile balance band get to experience genuine power growth rather than hitting a balance ceiling where additional content provides no felt progression.

The infinite tower is the right vehicle for this because traditional ARPG content has a finite "ceiling" (the hardest dungeon, the final boss) where overpowering becomes meaningless. An infinite tower has no ceiling, so increasing power has perpetual value.

Why this works in successful ARPGs
This pattern has clear precedent. Worth understanding it because the precedents inform both what works and what fails:

Diablo 3's Greater Rifts. Greater Rifts scale infinitely, with monster power increasing per level. Player power scales through paragon levels and gear upgrades. The relationship between these two scaling curves determines how deep players can push. Diablo 3 explicitly tunes these curves so that better-geared players push further, creating the "what GR can I push to?" gameplay loop that's been the game's primary endgame for years.

Path of Exile's mapping system. Maps come in tiers (T1 through T17 in recent versions), with each tier representing harder content. Player power scales through gear and passive tree investment. Players push toward maps where their power is matched to the content. The most engaged players push past where their power is comfortable, accepting tighter margins for the satisfaction of completing harder content.

Last Epoch's Monolith echo system. Echoes can be repeated at increasing corruption levels, scaling monster power. Players scale gear power through legendary potential and crafting. The relationship is similar — better gear lets you push higher corruption, which gives you access to better gear.

Lost Ark's Abyssal Dungeons and Argos raids. Item level requirements gate content access. Players scale their item level through gear improvements; content tiers require ever-higher item levels. This is more discrete than infinite scaling but operates on the same principle.

In all these cases, the gear-to-content power ratio is tuned to feel like progression rather than ceiling. Players who invest more time become measurably more powerful, and the content lets them feel that power.

Why the failure modes matter
The pattern works but it has failure modes that are worth being explicit about because your design choices will determine which failure modes you're vulnerable to.

Failure mode 1: Power creep across seasons. If gear power scales faster than monster power within a season, what happens between seasons? If gear and monsters both reset, you're fine — each season starts fresh with the gradient calibrated correctly. If gear carries over but monsters reset, players become trivially overpowered in subsequent seasons' baseline content. If monsters carry over but gear resets, players feel weak at the start of new seasons.

Your monthly reincarnation cycle naturally resets both — you generate new content and new gear each season. This protects you from cross-season power creep. Good.

Failure mode 2: Content irrelevance. If gear power scales much faster than monster scaling, all non-tower content becomes trivially easy as players progress. Story content, dungeons, world bosses — anything that's not the tower becomes "press attack to win" once gear power exceeds the content's design power.

This is what happens in Diablo 3 — once you're at Greater Rift 100+, the campaign and standard rifts are completely trivial. Players essentially only do Greater Rifts because everything else is meaningless.

Whether this is a problem depends on your design intent. If the tower is supposed to be the dominant endgame activity, content irrelevance for other activities is acceptable. If you want all endgame activities to remain engaging, you need to either scale them somehow or accept that the tower will eclipse them.

Failure mode 3: Power required for further power. The loop works as long as gear acquisitions actually unlock the next tower level. If at some point the gear gradient slows down (you've maxed out most slots, only minor optimizations remain), or the tower difficulty scaling outpaces what additional gear can provide, players hit a wall. This is the "I can't push past tower level 287" frustration where progress stops being satisfying.

Diablo 3 hits this — Greater Rift pushing eventually plateaus as gear becomes maxed and only paragon level growth helps, which is much slower than gear-driven growth. Players accept this as the final ceiling but it does end the active progression loop.

Failure mode 4: The hardcore gradient steepens to exclusion. If gear-to-monster ratio is tuned for the dedicated grinder, casual players might find the standard content becomes trivial too quickly. They blow through the season's intended content without feeling challenged, then face the tower without the optimization mindset that makes it satisfying.

This is partially solved by your 85th percentile balance band — content within the standard endgame is balanced for the bulk of players, with the tower being where the hardcore gradient kicks in. But you have to make sure the transition is well-designed.

Failure mode 5: Optimization treadmill. If gear power per level scales fast enough, the game becomes about optimization rather than experience. Players stop engaging with content for the content's sake and engage purely for the gear it provides, which lets them engage with harder content, which provides more gear, ad infinitum. This is fine for players who love optimization but exhausting for those who don't.

Your monthly reset helps here — the optimization treadmill resets each month rather than compounding indefinitely. Players who don't love optimization can stop when their engagement satisfies them; the treadmill doesn't punish them by making last month's investment worthless because they're moving to a new character anyway.

What the math actually needs to look like
Worth being concrete about the relationship between gear power and monster scaling because the specific numbers matter:

If gear power per level is +5% and monster power per level is +3%, the gap is +2% per level. Over 100 levels of pushing, players become roughly 100% more effective relative to content (cumulatively). This is generous and produces strong "I'm getting stronger" feel.

If gear power per level is +5% and monster power per level is +4%, the gap is +1% per level. Over 100 levels, players become roughly 65% more effective. This is moderate growth — still feels rewarding but doesn't dominate.

If gear power per level is +5% and monster power per level is +5.5%, gear actually falls behind. Each level becomes slightly harder than the last. This produces a death-spiral feel where players eventually can't push further and feel they're being punished for engagement.

The Diablo 3 Greater Rift curve is closer to the first scenario, with strong gear growth allowing dramatic progression. Path of Exile's mapping is closer to the second scenario, with more gradual relative growth. Both work; they produce different play feels.

For your design, the choice depends on what tower experience you want. If you want the tower to be a place where dedicated players feel meaningfully more powerful with each push, lean toward the first scenario. If you want the tower to remain challenging at every level with progress feeling earned through optimization, lean toward the second.

What "infinite tower" actually requires
A few things need to be true for an infinite tower to work well:

The content has to be procedurally varied. If every floor is the same encounter type, the tower becomes a stat checker — purely whether your gear can handle the math. Players lose interest. Floors need varied enemy compositions, varied layouts, varied environmental modifiers, varied objectives. Your engine's procedural generation capabilities support this.

The content has to scale qualitatively, not just quantitatively. If floor 50 is "the same monsters but with more HP and damage," that's boring. If floor 50 introduces new monster types, new environmental hazards, new combat dynamics, that's interesting. The qualitative escalation can mostly happen in early floors with quantitative scaling dominating in deep floors, but you need the qualitative layer for the tower to feel like a journey rather than a treadmill.

Failure has to be acceptable. In an infinite tower, players will eventually fail. The failure design matters — do they lose progress, lose nothing, lose a fraction? Lost Ark's Tower of Trials lets you continue from where you died with reduced rewards. Diablo 3's Greater Rifts let you fail and try again at the same level. Failures shouldn't feel punishing or the tower becomes anxiety-inducing.

Rewards have to scale. Gear acquired at floor 100 has to be meaningfully better than gear acquired at floor 50, or the depth doesn't matter. This is where your specification-driven gear generation does work — gear specifications can be calibrated for each floor or each floor range.

There has to be a metric players care about. Floor reached, time to clear floor X, leaderboard ranking, achievement progression. Pure depth needs to be visible and meaningful, or it becomes effort without recognition.

How this interacts with your monthly cycle
The monthly cycle is interesting in the context of an infinite tower because it creates a fundamental tension worth resolving:

Tension: deep tower runs require time investment that exceeds the monthly cycle. If a hardcore player is pushing toward tower floor 500 over a season, but the season ends at day 30 and resets the character entirely, what was the point? The deep optimization that infinite tower rewards is exactly the kind of investment that monthly reset destroys.

A few possible resolutions:

Resolution A: Each season's tower is self-contained. A new tower in each season's content. Floor depth achieved is the season's accomplishment, recognized in the season's leaderboards and achievements, then the season ends. Players engage with each season's tower for as much depth as they can achieve in the month. This preserves the cycle's reset philosophy.

Resolution B: Tower achievement persists across seasons. The tower itself is meta-cyclic. Players push depth that's recorded permanently. Each season, they bring a new character to the tower and either continue from their previous depth or restart, depending on design. This makes the tower a long-term progression layer above the monthly cycle.

Resolution C: The tower exists in the Throne, not the Wars. Using your Heroic Spirit framework — the Throne contains a permanent tower that any summoned Spirit can ascend. Each month's new Servant can push as deep as the Master can support them. Achievement is at the Master/account level, not the Servant/character level.

Option C is probably the cleanest fit for your design. The tower becomes a metagame layer where the player's accumulated knowledge and skill (not their specific character's gear) determines depth. Each new Servant brings their own loadout but the player's expertise in tower mechanics persists.

This also resolves the "what happens to my hardcore player at month end" question. The hardcore player who pushed floor 287 in season 3 carries that achievement forward into season 4, even though season 4's Servant is a different character. The tower is a continuous challenge across seasons.

What this means for engine work
Adding an infinite tower with gear-favored scaling has implications for content generation:

The tower needs its own content generation pipeline. Procedural floors with varied enemy compositions, scaling mechanics, and reward structures. This is substantial engine work — probably 4-6 weeks of focused development if you're building it from scratch, less if you can extend existing procedural systems.

Tower-specific gear scaling. Gear acquired in the tower might scale differently than gear acquired in the season's regular content. Tower gear is calibrated for the steeper scaling curve, while season content gear is calibrated for the 85th percentile balance band. These might need different generation paths.

Cross-season tower mechanics. If you go with Resolution C (Throne-resident tower), you need persistence systems for tower progression. Player account tracks deepest floor, achievements, leaderboard position. Cross-season metadata that survives the monthly reset.

Leaderboards and social features. Infinite towers without competitive recognition feel hollow. Players need to see how their depth compares to others, which requires social infrastructure (leaderboards, friend comparisons, possibly seasonal tower events).

