A few things make this design move powerful:

The casual player gets shareable wins. A player whose schedule doesn't permit serious endgame pushing can still have moments worth sharing. They optimize their build, choose their run carefully, and have a great three minutes that produces a shareable spirit guide assessment. Their accomplishment is real even if their total hours are modest.

The hardcore player gets a different kind of competition. Cumulative metrics reward time investment, which the hardcore already win by definition. Peak performance metrics introduce a different axis where time investment helps but doesn't determine the outcome. Hardcore players can compete on whether their best moments are better than other hardcore players' best moments, not just on whether they've played more.

The metric is naturally screenshotable. "Spirit guide noticed your KPM spike at 127 during tower floor 47" is a single piece of evidence that captures the moment. It's a unit of social media content — screenshots, video clips, replay highlights — that doesn't require extensive context to be impressive.

The competition can be granular. Peak metrics aren't just "did you do X" but "how good was your X." This produces fine-grained competition rather than binary accomplishment. Two players with the same content cleared at different KPM rates have different peak performances. The leaderboard becomes much more populated with meaningful positions.

It rewards build craft, not just time. A well-built character at moderate gear can produce KPM spikes that exceed a poorly-built character at high gear. The metric rewards the kind of optimization that's interesting to think about rather than the kind that requires accumulation. This serves the build-craft audience that auto-combat already attracts.

