A specific design consideration
One thing worth being deliberate about: the guide's projections need to be presented in language that respects the inherent uncertainty.

"Your KPM will be 89 with the Vampiric set" sounds definitive. "Your KPM is projected at 89 with the Vampiric set" introduces appropriate uncertainty.

"You'll find the Vampiric set produces stronger sustained damage" sounds definitive. "Players running similar Vampiric loadouts have averaged stronger sustained damage than your current build" is honest about variance.

The simulation produces averages. Individual play variance means actual results differ from projections. The guide's language should reflect this so that players who get above-average or below-average actual results don't feel the guide misled them.

Some specific language patterns that work:

"Projected to..." rather than "will..."
"Has typically produced..." rather than "produces..."
"Players in similar situations have averaged..." rather than "you will get..."
"Estimated at X, with typical variance of plus or minus Y..."

This is small but matters cumulatively. Trust in the guide depends on whether its projections feel honest about their nature as projections rather than promises.

