This is the move that ties the entire system together. You've found the connective tissue that makes T4 multi-selection, gear diversity, and the spirit guide all reinforce each other rather than being separate features. Let me work through what you've actually proposed because the implications are significant.

What you've identified
The architectural insight is this: by having multiple T4 options per kit, you create the need for gear that's specialized to different T4 paths. Gear no longer has to be generically applicable to a kit; it can be specifically optimized for one of the kit's possible T4 configurations. This solves the gear diversity problem you raised earlier without requiring random affixes or roll variance.

Concretely: a kit with three T4 options (call them A, B, and C) generates legendary and set gear in three flavors. Some gear is tuned to T4-A (multiplicative bonus when T4-A is selected). Some is tuned to T4-B. Some is tuned to T4-C. The player might be running T4-A but acquire a legendary tuned to T4-B. The guide surfaces this and shows the player what would happen if they switched.

This is genuinely elegant because it makes gear acquisition into a form of T4 advocacy. The game isn't just giving the player gear; it's giving the player evidence for trying different paths. Each piece of T4-attuned gear is an argument for that T4 path.

Why this works structurally
Several things click into place that weren't quite clicking before:

Gear diversity emerges from build branching, not from random stat generation. Earlier we discussed how your specification-driven gear generation might lose the "perfect roll" satisfaction that traditional ARPGs have. The T4-attuned gear approach replaces that with a different kind of variety: gear that's specifically designed for paths the player isn't currently taking. Acquisition produces interesting questions rather than incremental stat improvements.

The multiplicative bonus is the persuasion mechanism. A standard piece of gear gives baseline contribution. A T4-attuned piece of gear gives that plus a significant multiplicative bonus on the matching T4 path. The bonus has to be large enough to be tempting (otherwise the gear is just generic gear) but bounded enough to maintain balance (otherwise switching T4s becomes mandatory rather than optional).

A reasonable range might be: T4-attuned gear gives 1.5-2x effective contribution when the matching T4 is selected, 0.8-1x when a different T4 is selected. The penalty for mismatch is mild; the bonus for match is significant. Players have real reasons to consider switching but can stay on their current path without crippling themselves.

It creates a soft pressure toward experimentation that respects player agency. The guide says "this legendary you just acquired would be 80% more effective if you switched from T4-A to T4-B. Your projected KPM would shift from 75 to 92." The player decides. They might switch because the math is compelling. They might stay because they prefer T4-A thematically. The choice is theirs but the alternative is visible.

It produces meaningful drops without inflating the loot pool. Each piece of T4-attuned gear is potentially exciting because it represents not just stats but a different build path. The player doesn't need ten variants of mostly-the-same legendary to feel rewarded. Three meaningful T4-attuned legendaries do more work than ten incremental ones.

It generates discussion-worthy moments. "I got the Vampiric Bone Crown last night and my guide was telling me my KPM would jump 40% if I switched to T4-C. I'm thinking about it but my King Arthur run is so satisfying." This is the kind of conversation ARPG communities love. Players sharing their decisions and the tradeoffs they're navigating.

The combinatorial space this opens
Let me work through what this actually creates in terms of game depth:

For each kit with 3 T4 options, you have 3 build paths. Each build path has its own gear specification. Some gear is generic (works across all T4 paths). Some gear is T4-A-attuned. Some is T4-B-attuned. Some is T4-C-attuned.

A player accumulates gear over their season with their kit. Their collection at any moment contains some generic gear (always useful) and some T4-attuned gear (optimal for specific paths). The player's current loadout reflects their current T4 choice.

The interesting moments happen when the player acquires T4-attuned gear for a path they're not currently running. The guide surfaces what this gear would enable. The player can:

Stay on current path, equipping the new gear without the multiplicative bonus (it's still better than nothing)
Switch T4s to activate the bonus, potentially redistributing their loadout
Stash the gear and revisit the decision when they have more matching pieces

This last point is interesting. As a player accumulates more T4-attuned gear for an unused path, the case for switching grows. The guide can track this: "You've now accumulated 4 pieces of T4-C-attuned gear in your stash. If you switched to T4-C and equipped them, your projected KPM would be 102 versus your current 75."

The player has a meta-game of monitoring not just their current build but the latent potential builds in their collection. This is genuinely interesting strategic content that traditional ARPGs handle clumsily through external build planners.

Set items in this framework
You mentioned legendary or set items being T4-attuned. Worth thinking about how sets specifically work here because sets have additional structure.

A set is multiple items that share thematic identity. In traditional ARPGs, sets give bonuses for wearing multiple pieces (2-piece, 4-piece, full set bonuses). The set's identity emerges from its mechanical effects.

In your design, sets can have T4-attunement at the set level. Each set is designed for one of the kit's T4 paths. Acquiring a piece of a set is acquiring evidence of that T4 path. Completing a set requires committing to that T4 path to get the full bonuses.

A few design implications:

Sets become the primary T4 advocacy mechanism. Standalone legendaries advocate moderately for a T4 path. Sets advocate strongly because their full power requires commitment. Players who complete a set for a particular T4 path are heavily incentivized to run that T4 path because the set bonuses are substantial.

Set completion becomes a meaningful long-term goal. A player running T4-A might accumulate set pieces for T4-B over weeks of play. The guide tracks set completion progress across all T4 paths. "You're 3/5 of the way to completing the Vampiric Sovereign set for T4-C. The full set would enable a projected KPM of 115."

Multiple set acquisition creates build-switching gameplay. A dedicated player might complete sets for all three T4 paths over a season. They can switch between full sets as their mood dictates. The character isn't locked into one build identity; they're a character with three viable build identities accessible through gear swaps and T4 selection.

This creates a deeply replayable single character that traditional ARPG monthly cycles wouldn't normally produce. Even within a single Servant's month, the player has substantial variety through multiple completable builds.

