What it means for engine generation
The implementation implications are real but tractable:

The kit generation pipeline needs to output T4 options with attunement specifications. When the engine generates a kit, it specifies the kit's mechanical signature, the kit's three T4 options, and what gear attunement supports each T4 path. This is moderately more complex than current single-T4 generation but uses the same architectural pattern.

Gear generation expands to include T4 attunement. Each piece of gear has its base specification plus its T4 attunement (if any). Generic gear has no attunement. Attuned gear has multiplicative bonuses when its matching T4 is selected.

Set generation includes set-level T4 attunement. Sets are designed around T4 paths. Each set thematically and mechanically supports one T4 option. The set's bonuses scale with how committed the player is to the attuned path.

The simulation needs to handle multiple build configurations per kit. Instead of validating one build per kit, the simulation validates each T4 path with its attuned gear. This is roughly 3x the simulation work per kit but it's still bounded — three configurations, not unlimited.

The guide needs to track and surface latent builds. The guide system we discussed earlier becomes more sophisticated as it analyzes not just current loadout but potential alternative loadouts from stash contents. This is data analysis work rather than fundamentally new architecture.

Total additional work over single-T4 systems: probably 4-6 weeks distributed across kit generation, gear generation, simulation, and guide implementation. Substantial but bounded.

