Worth being honest about: this design is more complex than traditional ARPG gear systems. Players engaging with it have to understand T4 attunement, multiplicative bonuses, set bonuses, build switching, and acquisition planning.

For your target audience (ARPG veterans, strategy game players, depth-seekers) this complexity is a feature. They want depth and the layered systems provide it.

For more casual audiences, the complexity could be barrier. A player who just wants to play and have fun might be overwhelmed by the strategic decisions the design generates.

The guide mitigates this significantly. New players can ignore the strategic complexity and just play what the guide surfaces as currently optimal. They can engage with the depth gradually as they become comfortable. The guide handles the cognitive load that the depth would otherwise impose.

But the guide can't fully eliminate the complexity. Players need to understand what T4 attunement means, what set completion implies, what the tradeoffs of switching builds are. Some onboarding work is necessary to introduce these concepts gracefully.

Your spirit guide as active companion is well-positioned for this. The guide can introduce concepts contextually as they become relevant. When the player first acquires T4-attuned gear, the guide explains what attunement means. When they accumulate enough for a set, the guide introduces set mechanics. The complexity is revealed progressively as the player encounters it.

